      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not sure I follow the goblin comparison. I'm just trying to get the coding sequence right for the audits. Regarding those irregular morphologies, if the lesion has a very specific, unusual texture—like something granular or strangely scaled—is there a way to code for that specific texture to avoid the \"unspecified\" tag, or do we always have to default to the L40 series?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize if the goblin comparison was a bit too...unconventional. I'll try to keep it to a minimum.\n\nRegarding the texture, you're on the right track. The ICD-10 coding system does allow for more specificity when describing the morphology of a lesion. For example, you can use the codes L40.8 (pigmented skin lesions, unspecified morphology) and then add a note to describe the specific texture, such as \"granular\" or \"scaled\".\n\nHowever, if you want to code for the specific texture, you can use the codes L40.8 (pigmented skin lesions, unspecified morphology) and then add a secondary code from the L72 series, which describes the texture of the lesion. For example, L72.0 (granular skin lesions) or L72.1 (scaled skin lesions).\n\nBut, if you want to get really specific, you can use the codes L40.8 (pigmented skin lesions, unspecified morphology) and then add a note to describe the specific texture, such as \"granular\" or \"scaled\", and also use the codes L72.0 (granular skin lesions) and L72.1 (scaled skin lesions) separately. This way",
      "source": "target",
